# Main

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

The `main` element holds the content that is unique to a page, such as the household guide itself. It lets keyboard and screen reader users jump past the repeated header and navigation and land on the content.

Use it on standalone pages that Civica or a household publishes, such as a printed or shared guide. Home Assistant dashboards do not give an author this element. The [Skip link](skip-link.html) page owns the link that points to it.

This page covers:

- Main
  - `<main>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A page with a main region has, from top to bottom:

1. A header with the site or household name and the navigation, outside `main`.
2. `main`, holding the page heading and the content that is only on this page.
3. A footer, outside `main`.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-main"><code>&lt;main&gt;</code></h3>

The `main` element has no attributes of its own and no visual style: browsers draw it as a plain block. Its rules:

- Use one visible `main` on a page. A second one must be hidden.
- Do not put it inside `article`, `aside`, `footer`, `header` or `nav`.
- Keep the header, the navigation and the footer outside it.
- Use `main` rather than a `div` with a `main` role.
- When a skip link points to it, give it an `id` and `tabindex="-1"`, so the link can move focus to it.

**In a Home Assistant dashboard.** A dashboard author cannot add `main`. A Markdown card removes it and shows the tags as visible text, and this version of Home Assistant draws no `main` element in its own pages. Rely on Home Assistant’s page structure, and give each card a clear heading. In a Markdown card, use `##` and `###` headings in order. See [Markdown Card](markdown-card.html).

**What the Civica theme sets.** Nothing. `main` has no style to theme. The page ground comes from `primary-background-color`.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- One landmark lets people reach the content directly from the landmarks list or a skip link.
- It adds nothing visually.
- It does not replace headings. Without a page heading, people still cannot tell what the page is about.
- It helps only on a page you control. A dashboard author cannot add or label it, so the structure that people rely on there is Home Assistant’s own.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use one `main` on each standalone page and keep the page heading at its start, in the same place on every page. Put the header, navigation and footer outside it. Use a skip link that points to it on any page with repeated navigation.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the landmark states what the content is.
- 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks: people can skip the repeated header and navigation.
- 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): after a skip, the focused content must not be hidden behind a fixed header.

One main region and a heading in a consistent place keep the page easy to follow.

